.TH "RTCSESSIONDESCRIPTION.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
RTCSessionDescription.toJSON \- RTCSessionDescription: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]RTCSessionDescription\f[R] interface returns a JSON\-serializable
plain object representing the \f[CR]RTCSessionDescription\f[R] object.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when
an \f[CR]RTCSessionDescription\f[R] object is encountered during JSON
serialization.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A JSON\-serializable plain object containing the following properties:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]type\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]sdp\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example creates an \f[CR]RTCSessionDescription\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] description = \f[B]new\f[R] RTCSessionDescription({ type: \(dqrollback\(dq, sdp: \(dq\(dq });

\f[B]const\f[R] json = description.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
console.log(json.type); \f[I]// Same value as description.type\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]RTCSessionDescription\f[R] object from the previous example is
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(description));
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqrollback\(dq,
  \(dqsdp\(dq: \(dq\(dq
}
.EE
